How elbow moment varies during a pitch
The next plot shows how elbow varus/valgus moment changes over the recorded duration of a pitch. Curves are aligned by each trial’s relative time and summarized with the median and interquartile range. This is an elbow loading measure, not a direct measurement of stress on shoulder tissue.


Code
moment_profile &lt;- plot_data |&gt;
  group_by(session_pitch) |&gt;
  mutate(
    pitch_progress = round(
      (time - min(time)) / (max(time) - min(time)) * 100
    )
  ) |&gt;
  group_by(session_pitch, pitch_progress) |&gt;
  summarise(elbow_moment_y = median(elbow_moment_y), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  group_by(pitch_progress) |&gt;
  summarise(
    median_moment = median(elbow_moment_y),
    lower_moment = quantile(elbow_moment_y, 0.25),
    upper_moment = quantile(elbow_moment_y, 0.75),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

ggplot(moment_profile, aes(x = pitch_progress, y = median_moment)) +
  geom_ribbon(
    aes(ymin = lower_moment, ymax = upper_moment),
    fill = "#8FC1D4",
    alpha = 0.5
  ) +
  geom_line(color = "#176B87", linewidth = 1) +
  labs(
    x = "Relative recorded pitch duration (%)",
    y = "Elbow varus (+) / valgus (-) moment (Nm)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 13)








Figure 2: Median elbow varus/valgus moment over the relative recorded duration of a pitch; shading shows the interquartile range across pitches.





This time-course plot adds context by showing when elbow moment tends to rise or fall over a pitch, while the shaded band shows how much the middle half of trials varies at each point. It helps locate phases of greater typical elbow loading and compare consistency across pitches; it does not measure shoulder tissue stress or establish injury risk.
